<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nag Hammadi library</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chenoboskion Manuscripts, Gnostic Gospels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nag Hammadi library is a collection of early Christian and Gnostic texts discovered near the Upper Egyptian town of Nag Hammadi in 1945. Twelve leather-bound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> codices and a tractate from a thirteenth, buried in a sealed jar, were found by a farmer, Muhammed al-Samman, and others; they hold 52 mostly Gnostic treatises in Coptic, three works of the Corpus Hermeticum and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> date from the 3rd and 4th centuries, and among them is the only complete text of the Gospel of Thomas. Some scholars hold that they belonged to a nearby Pachomian monastery. The codices are now housed in the Coptic Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library.jpg" alt="Nag Hammadi library" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Evangelio_de_Tomás-Gospel_of_Thomas-_Codex_II_Manuscritos_de_Nag_Hammadi-The_Nag_Hammadi_manuscripts.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Bible, the lives and sayings of the desert fathers, the writings of the abbot Shenoute of Atripe, the greatest Coptic author, and the Gnostic texts of the <b>Nag Hammadi library</b>, a collection of 4th-century Coptic books, including the Gospel of Thomas, found in Upper Egypt in 1945.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gnostic texts of the <b>Nag Hammadi library</b> are written mainly in Sahidic, with traces of Lycopolitan, a dialect much used for Gnostic and Manichaean translations.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
